Z||000000||ka4nkf||04-09-2001||12:41 AM||||I have been reading in you cave about brakes and you have a very good descripton of how the brakes work. You say and GM also that you should use the E brake often to keep the rear brakes adjusted. I understand this and how it works, but tell me what keeps the front brakes in adjustment. If it is the porportional valve(sorry i don't know how to spell it) then the rear should be the same.<BR>Just curious, because I don't know a lot about brakes.<P>Thanks<BR>Don<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||85Josh||04-09-2001||06:28 PM||||The front brake are essentially self-adjusting. Disk brakes actually don't need adjusted, they do it automatically when you stop. The reason that REAR disks need adjusted is because of the ratcheting screw mechanism that applies the e-brake.<P>------------------<BR>85 2M4(Lil' Bit)||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||04-09-2001||07:19 PM||||The front pistons go out as far as they need to then they retact ever so slightly in the seal inside the bore. The outer seal that you normally see is only a dust boot. There is no return spring in the front caliper so once they go out they only retract the mount that the seal was deformed by applying them.<P>The rear caliper does have a return spring in the piston and needs the handbrake to operate in order to work. These calipers actually have multiple springs inside. If the handbrake is never used it is posible that the rear brakes may not grab as they should or they will need more pedal to operate. In the case of a bad parking brake system, either internal or external to the caliper, you could also have them drag on the rotor. <p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-09-2001).]||||reg||1
